A||N||7||Monkeyman||Question about transferring programs||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-28-2001||06:17 PM||||My wife has a program on her computer at work (Print Shop Deluxe III) that she'd like to transfer to her computer at home.  She doesn't have a CD burner at work.  Obviously, we can't network with that one either.  I'm guessing that the program will be way too big to fit on a floppy.  What are her options?  Is she just SOL?<P>If there isn't any way for her to copy that program to her computer at home, is there anyone who has that version of Print Shop (we have most of the rest of the versions, but she happens to like that particular one) they'd like to let us "borrow"?  Of course this would be for educational purposes only.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PontiacAPE.jpg">    <BR>Monkeyman||||reg||5
Z||000001||maryjane||10-28-2001||08:00 PM||||Is that a Mindscape program? I've got something here called Printmaster Gold Deluxe Version 3.0 Publishing Suite. It's on a silver cd with yellow label. If it's what you want, it's yours. Email me or PM.<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||10-28-2001||08:19 PM||||Get a parallel port transfer cable. There is a utility in windows for transfers, but I just use an old DOS program called LapLink. Copy the files to your laptop and bring it home and then copy it to your other PC. Only problem is that you will have to make several attempts because nearly all programs add some dll files and they are hiding out in other directories...I mean folders. I usually look in the bargin bin of software at Wal-Mart and Office Max for older stuff like that. Picked up Print Shop 4 for $5.00 a while back, I wish that I could find it now for the new PC.||||reg||1
Z||000003||btoth||10-29-2001||09:23 AM||||If you really wanted to... you could always just split the files over multiple floppies.  (When was the last time I used a floppy???)  Anyway, is the program zipped?  Or is it on a CD?<P>Either way, if you use WinZip or PowerArchiver (my pick) you can add all of the files to a new ZIP file and save it to a floppy... if it's too big for the floppy, it should ask if you want to split it over more than one floppy.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||10-29-2001||04:10 PM||||Hmm...Didn't know I could split it between floppys (or is it floppies?).  How would it load in her computer at home?  I'm not worried about the AutoRun, but...  And I never thought about zipping it, either.  Good idea.  I doubt she has WinZip (never heard of the other) at work, but that should be easy enough to transfer from my computer to hers as I have a CD burner (and she has a CD ROM at work). <P>Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||10-29-2001||04:22 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.winzip.com" TARGET=_blank>www.winzip.com</A>   (tiny download, I think)<P>The program has a Help, so you can look up the step-by-step instructions on how to span (or split) a program to multiple floppies.<P>Then, as long as the home computers has it installed as well, you just put the first floppy in and double-click the file (which would be something .zip) and the program will tell you what to do with the rest of the disks.  Usually something like "insert last disk", then "insert disk 2" etc.||||reg||7
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-29-2001||04:23 PM||||Im sure it is too big to fit on floppys (unless you have a hundred of them)   <P>Any chance she can get the "loan" of it for the night from work?<P>||||reg||1
